This directory contains various "canned" versions of h/posix/limits.h.  The
comment at the top of each file notes which operating system and hardware the
file has been tested on.

If one of these is directly usable in your environment, then copy or link it
to h/posix/limits.h.

Otherwise, copy the one that most closely approximates your configuration into
h/posix/limits.h and edit it appropriately.  Once it works, please mail a copy
of it to "ipw@crseo.ucsb.edu" so we can incorporate it into the standard
distribution.  Be sure that the comment at the top of the file adequately
describes your particular operating system.

NOTE!  These files should contain ONLY those macros that are UNIQUE to the
       POSIX <limits.h>.  Also, macros which are unused by IPW need not be
       defined.

macros:
	ARG_MAX		maximum length of argument for exec() in bytes,
				including environ data
	CHILD_MAX	maximum number of offspring per user ID
	CLK_TCK		number of clock ticks per second
	FCHR_MAX	maximum size of a file in bytes
	LOCK_MAX	maximum number of system lock table entries
	LINK_MAX	maximum number of links to a file
	MAX_INPUT	maximum number of characters allowed for terminal
				input
	NAME_MAX	maximum number of characters in a file name
	NGROUPS_MAX	maximum number of simultaneous supplementary group IDs
				per process
	OPEN_LIMIT	maximum number of files that one process can have open
				at any given time.  Use 1024.
	OPEN_MAX	minimum number of files open concurrently guaranteed
	PASS_MAX	maximum number of characters in a password
	PATH_MAX	maximum number of characters in a path name
	PID_MAX		maximum value for a process ID
	PIPE_BUF	maximum number of bytes that is guaranteed to be
				atomic when writing to a pipe
	PIPE_MAX	maximum number of bytes that can be written to a pipe
				in a single write operation
	PROC_MAX	maximum number of simultaneous processes
	STD_BLK		standard number of bytes in a physical I/O block;
				also, the maximum number of bytes guaranteed
				to be transferred in atomic I/O actions
	SYS_NMLN	maximum number of characters for strings returned by
				the uname() function
	SYS_OPEN	maximum number of files that can be open on the system
				at any one time
	UID_MAX		maximum value for a user or group ID
	WORD_BIT	number of bits in a word or in data of type int

